  "dom_id": "step-q3875",
  "name": "Question 15",
  "problem": "Find the solution of ${x}^{2}\\le -9x.$",
  "worked_solution": "To solve this inequality using the graphical method, we first need to move all of the numbers to the left-hand side so that the right-hand side is $0.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & \\le -9x \\\\ {x}^{2}+9x & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we want to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola $y={x}^{2}+9x$ lies below the $x$-axis or touches this axis. Let's find the roots of the parabola by factoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9x & =0 \\\\ x(x+9) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the roots are $x=-9$ and $x=0,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14004892/assets/eacd8f468f34bea6eec93afd32bbeee452a3a6435342035f7c45c3578f40f92f.png)\n\nSince we are interested in solving \n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+9x\\le 0,\n$$\n\n we look for the part of the parabola that is below the $x$-axis or touches this axis. \n\nBased on the graph, the solution is $-9\\le x\\le 0.$ \n\nUsing interval notation, we write $x\\in [-9,0].$",
